ttywish is a Perl script that tells you the wishes made in a ttyrec
(and when they were made). It may be of limited usefulness (perhaps you
want a list of wishes made for a YAAP?) but someone may appreciate it,
so I present it to rgrn.

Earlier today I wrote it in an attempt to really learn the ttyrec
format. It started as an attempt to rewrite Simon Tatham's ttygrep,
available at:

http://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/ipbt/

ttygrep's problem is that searching is constrained to a single frame..
it isn't actually that much of a problem unless you're trying to match
user input which comes at about one character per frame (like, say, a
person typing in a wish). Instead of trying to match each frame's data,
I instead ran the data through Term::VT102 (a handy Perl module), which
then allows you to match each row. It works great, except *incredibly*
slowly. On an ordinary ttyrec, ttygrep2 was at least 800x slower than
ttygrep. Seven minutes versus half a second. Yeah, that bad.

On the other hand, ttygrep2 'For what do you wish\?.*' worked very
nicely: it tells you each wish made in the ttyrec. The only (other)
problem is that it matches once per keystroke.. so for each letter in
the item you wish for, that's one match.

So I started working on a special-purpose tool to sanely (and,
hopefully, quickly) extract the wishes from the ttyrec. Originally it
was based on ttygrep2 and worked like a charm (but still slow as hell).
Term::VT102, while a life saver, is incredibly slow for a task like
this, and had to go. After a few setbacks, ttywish was finished. It,
like Simon's ttygrep, takes about half a second on the ordinary
ascension ttyrec.

For each wish it prints out precisely what the user requested (you
don't get to see what they actually received though.. anything more
than "a scroll labeled THARR" would be rather nontrivial) and on what
turn they made the wish. A future version may include a switch for
ttyrec frame instead of turn number (and then support ttygrep's -j
switch to number frames as though the many input ttyrecs were
concatenated to form a large one).

ttywish should correctly handle:
* backspaces ("For what do you wish? 2 bl^H^H^H3 blessed scrolls of
charging")
* extremely long wishes which wrap to the next line
* ^P should produce no false positives (though I haven't fully tested
this..)
* "For what do you wish? hits!"

The script version 1.2 follows; it's also available (perhaps updated)
at http://sartak.katron.org/nh/ttywish

Shawn M Moore


